/-!
# Cohen–Gabber finite power-series presentations

Source: Illusie–Laszlo–Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé IV, Theorem 2.1.1,
Remarks 2.1.2, Theorem 4.2.2 and proof 4.2.5; Exposé V, 3.1.3; Stacks Project,
Algebra, Tag 032D for equal characteristic zero
(blueprint: lem-per-cohen-gabber-finite-presentations).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace IsLocalRing

/-- A complete Noetherian normal local domain with separably closed residue field has
Cohen–Gabber presentations, in equal characteristic without changing the domain and in
mixed characteristic after a finite injective extension. The two conjuncts spell out
these two characteristic cases. Their numbers of variables are respectively `d` and
`d - 1`, so are positive when `2 ≤ d`. Bijectivity of `ResidueField.map φ` asserts the
residue-field isomorphism for the very same local map `φ`. Reduction of `f` is
coefficientwise, and the étale map is the canonical localization of `φ`.

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, IV 2.1.1–2.1.12 and IV 4.2.2–4.2.5; V 3.1.3;
equal characteristic zero: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 032D.
(blueprint: lem-per-cohen-gabber-finite-presentations).

Proof (the ten steps of the DAG):
1. For a field or any complete DVR Λ, power-series Noetherianity, the domain lemma,
   and the unit criterion give Noetherian local domains S_j. The maximal ideal is
   generated by the j variables, together with a uniformizer in the DVR case, by
   the quotient-variables lemma. Completeness follows from the complete-base lemma.
   The generator bound gives dimension at most j or j+1. The strict chain obtained
   by successively killing variables, and finally the uniformizer in the DVR case,
   gives the reverse bound. These generators then prove regularity, also for j=0.
2. In equal characteristic zero, complete-local-domain finite normalization gives
   a power-series field subring with residue isomorphism. Integral injective
   dimension equality makes its number of variables d. The finite fraction-field
   extension is separable in characteristic zero. The finite-algebra étale-near-field
   lemma at the zero prime gives a nonzero f and an étale localization.
3. In characteristic p, for a reduced equidimensional complete local B choose a
   coefficient section and generic differential basis using the coefficient-field
   generic-differential-basis lemma, respecting a fixed perfect subfield if given.
   Choose a system of parameters τ_i. Formal normalization gives injectivity on
   every minimal component, so each τ_i is nonzero in each component fraction field.
4. Replace τ_i by u_i = τ_i^p(1+a_i). This preserves the radical parameter ideal,
   so formal normalization gives a finite injective local residue-isomorphism map
   from k[[u]]. Since du_i = τ_i^p da_i, the differential-cokernel lemma kills the
   generic relative differential module on every component. The generic-product
   clause of formal normalization then kills it over the generic base field.
5. The finite-algebra étale-near-field lemma, using torsion-freeness from formal
   normalization and step 4, gives g ≠ 0 with étale localization. Apply this to
   B=A in equal characteristic p, taking A'=A. In both equal-characteristic cases
   reduction modulo the coefficient field's zero maximal ideal preserves nonzero f.
6. In mixed characteristic use the reduced-special-fibre-cover lemma to obtain
   A → C finite injective, C complete normal local of dimension d, and a complete
   perfect-residue DVR W mapping to C with reduced equidimensional special fibre
   B=C/πC of dimension d-1. Steps 3–5 applied over the fixed perfect residue field
   give a compatible coefficient section and formal parameters for B.
7. The perfect-residue-coefficient-DVR lemma lifts this section to a complete DVR
   Λ → C with the same uniformizer. This map is injective since no power of π
   vanishes in the characteristic-zero domain C. Lift the special-fibre parameters
   and use the complete power-series universal property to obtain Λ[[T]] → C.
   Its reduction is the step-6 map, by the same universal property's uniqueness.
8. The lifted parameters and π generate a maximal-primary ideal. Formal normalization,
   case (b), now makes the lifted map finite, injective, local, and a residue-field
   isomorphism. Set A'=C and r=d-1. The finite inclusion A → C is from step 6.
9. The ideal πR is prime with quotient k'[[T]], a domain. Multiplication by π is
   injective coefficientwise. The regular-principal-prime-localization-DVR lemma
   makes R localized at πR a DVR. Its fibre algebra is the generic localization of
   B, whose differentials vanish by step 4. The finite-algebra étale-near-DVR lemma
   gives f outside πR. Thus f has nonzero coefficientwise reduction and the
   canonical map R[1/f] → C[1/f] is étale.
10. In equal characteristic Λ has the residue field of A. In mixed characteristic
    its residue field is finite over that of A and hence separably closed by the
    separably-closed finite-extension lemma. Step 1 supplies all intermediate
    power-series properties for this actual Λ. Finally d≥2 ensures r≥1. -/
theorem cohen_gabber_finite_presentations
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    [IsDomain A] [IsIntegrallyClosed A]
    [IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal A) A] [IsSepClosed (ResidueField A)]
    (d : ℕ) (hd : 2 ≤ d) (hdim : ringKrullDim A = (d : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    (ringChar A = ringChar (ResidueField A) →
      ∃ (Λ : Type u) (_ : Field Λ),
        IsSepClosed (ResidueField Λ) ∧
        ∃ (φ : MvPowerSeries (Fin d) Λ →+* A) (_ : IsLocalHom φ),
          Function.Injective φ ∧ φ.Finite ∧
          Function.Bijective (ResidueField.map φ) ∧
          (∃ f : MvPowerSeries (Fin d) Λ,
            MvPowerSeries.map (residue Λ) f ≠ 0 ∧
            (Localization.awayMap φ f).Etale) ∧
          ∀ j : ℕ, j ≤ d →
            IsRegularLocalRing (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ) ∧
            IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ))
              (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ) ∧
            ringKrullDim (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ) = (j : WithBot ℕ∞)) ∧
    (∀ p : ℕ, p.Prime → ringChar A = 0 → ringChar (ResidueField A) = p →
      ∃ (A' : Type u) (_ : CommRing A') (_ : IsLocalRing A')
        (_ : IsDomain A') (_ : IsIntegrallyClosed A') (ι : A →+* A'),
        Function.Injective ι ∧ ι.Finite ∧
        ∃ (Λ : Type u) (_ : CommRing Λ) (_ : IsDomain Λ)
          (_ : IsDiscreteValuationRing Λ),
          IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal Λ) Λ ∧
          ringChar (ResidueField Λ) = p ∧ IsSepClosed (ResidueField Λ) ∧
          ∃ (φ : MvPowerSeries (Fin (d - 1)) Λ →+* A') (_ : IsLocalHom φ),
            Function.Injective φ ∧ φ.Finite ∧
            Function.Bijective (ResidueField.map φ) ∧
            (∃ f : MvPowerSeries (Fin (d - 1)) Λ,
              MvPowerSeries.map (residue Λ) f ≠ 0 ∧
              (Localization.awayMap φ f).Etale) ∧
            ∀ j : ℕ, j ≤ d - 1 →
              IsRegularLocalRing (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ) ∧
              IsAdicComplete (maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ))
                (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ) ∧
              ringKrullDim (MvPowerSeries (Fin j) Λ) = (j + 1 : ℕ)) := by
  sorry

end IsLocalRing
